A||||14||loafer87gt||3.5 DOHC Shortstar Conversion||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||12-17-1999||02:53 PM||||Has anyone done a swap with one of these. Its essentially a Northstar with 2 cylinders lopped off. Right now its only being used for powering the Olds Intrigue. I think it would be an excellent engine for a swap as it loves to rev (6700 RPM redline), and is supposed to be very reliable, not to mention extremely sweet looking! I was planning on doing a 3.4 DOHC swap next year, but if anyone knows if the 3.5 will work I'd rather go with that. Also, anyone know what sort of price tag is on one of these things.||||reg||1
Z||000001||sluppy123||12-17-1999||05:30 PM||||I could be wrong but the "Shortstar" engine is only a 3.0 liter 200 HP DOHC V-6, and it's only in the Cadillac Catera? But I could be wrong-Brian||||reg||1
Z||000002||loafer87gt||12-17-1999||06:55 PM||||The Intrigue is standard with a premium 3.5-liter twin-cam V6 engine. The state-of-the-art 24-valve powerplant uses the latest in overhead cam technology to deliver the responsive rush of 215 horsepower when you need it most - on the highway. Designed and tuned exclusively for Intrigue, the V6 was derived from Aurora V8 technology and engineered using the most extensive computer testing in our history. The result is an aluminum construction that translates directly into enhanced driving confidence and control; the lighter weight helps Intrigue handle with agility and security. And with platinum-tipped spark plugs and long-life fluids, it's even got maintenance under control.* Overall, the 3.5-liter V6 twin-cam is smoother, lighter, and swifter. <P>The 3.5 liter V6 Intrigue engine builds on the advanced technology used for the Aurora 4.0 liter eight-cylinder engine while introducing several new features. <P>The 3.0 in the Catera is a 60 degree v6 whereas the Shortstar is a 90 degree. I wish I could find the weight of the 3.5, as it the block and heads are boht aluminum.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||william_thorniii||12-17-1999||07:09 PM||||Thought about is not at all the way I would describe it.  I have dreamed of this conversion since last Jan. at the Detroit Auto Show where I caught my first glimpse at the fact sheet of the Short*.  The reason for the confusion is that technically there are several versions of the Short* just like the N*.  The 3L is made from the Olds 4L N* and the 3.5L is made from the Caddy 4.6L N*.  <P>Unfortunately all of the great things about owning this engine make it %$#@%@$# to install.  Have you ever heard of OBD-II.  Well in 1996 all vehicles switched over to the system, while it helps out in many ways, it makes installation a nightmare.  Then again a * engine is essentially an electrical nightmare anyway.  So that naturally points to the TDC (3.4L).  The only problem is that it is famed to have carried over too many problems from its predecessor... the infamous Quad 4.  Although the * engines are evolutions of the Quad 4, they are MUCH improved in the mechanical reliability areas. <P>I am so excited though that someone else is pondering this switch.  In all reality, I would probably go all the way if I decided to go with this and get a pre-96 N*.  I have been researching DOHC engines with the intention of a Fiero swap for about a year now.  Needless to say, I am getting close to the purchasing stage.  Let me know what you decide to do about this.  <P>We definitely need to discuss this further... especially my complete dream a DOHC with forced induction.. gotta go now... getting too excited. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||12-17-1999||08:33 PM||||Super charged 3800's are nice||||reg||1
